00:12:38.711 --> 00:12:41.460
but think about paying

00:12:41.461 --> 00:12:45.580
a base time and distance
per mile and per minute,

00:12:45.581 --> 00:12:49.679
and then when demand is
much higher than supply,

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.930
the surge multiple goes above
one meaning that riders are

00:12:52.931 --> 00:12:55.320
paying that multiple of

00:12:55.321 --> 00:12:56.720
the normal time
and distance fare,

00:12:56.721 --> 00:12:57.750
and drivers are being paid

00:12:57.751 --> 00:12:59.915
that multiple of the normal time
and distance fare.

00:12:59.916 --> 00:13:01.900
I guess it's not quite
that simple but you

00:13:01.901 --> 00:13:03.940
can think about a 2x
surge multiple as being

00:13:03.941 --> 00:13:05.830
roughly doubling the price

00:13:05.831 --> 00:13:08.145
for riders and
the payment for drivers.

00:13:08.146 --> 00:13:11.410
So, we're going to talk

00:13:11.411 --> 00:13:14.970
today about how a
surge price might be

00:13:14.971 --> 00:13:16.800
calculated and so we're

00:13:16.801 --> 00:13:18.910
showing a heat map of
the surge multiple

00:13:18.911 --> 00:13:20.710
on the right-hand side
where darker purple

00:13:20.711 --> 00:13:22.590
means a higher surge multiple.

00:13:22.591 --> 00:13:30.980
So, the simplest matching

00:13:30.981 --> 00:13:33.870
algorithm that you could
think of would be to,

00:13:33.871 --> 00:13:36.780
as soon as a rider
requests a ride to simply

00:13:36.781 --> 00:13:40.505
dispatch the car that can get
to that rider the fastest.

00:13:40.506 --> 00:13:43.530
That's called the first
dispatch protocol and that's

00:13:43.531 --> 00:13:45.350
the dispatch protocol that was

00:13:45.351 --> 00:13:47.665
used in the simulation
that I showed you before.

00:13:47.666 --> 00:13:50.160
So, even this very simple type of

00:13:50.161 --> 00:13:54.905
dispatch protocol does lead
to these high efficiencies.

00:13:54.906 --> 00:13:57.540
That being said, there's
a variety of ways

00:13:57.541 --> 00:14:01.180
to decrease that
pickup time even more,

00:14:01.181 --> 00:14:03.680
and I'll show you
an example of one.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.350
But, before I do that,
I do just want to point

00:14:07.351 --> 00:14:09.560
out the fact that the key input

00:14:09.561 --> 00:14:11.910
for dispatch decisions is

00:14:11.911 --> 00:14:14.050
this prediction of travel time
and the road network,

00:14:14.051 --> 00:14:17.095
how long will this
pickup take for example.

00:14:17.096 --> 00:14:18.230
&gt;&gt; Question?

00:14:18.231 --> 00:14:19.055
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:14:19.056 --> 00:14:21.920
&gt;&gt; What do you
compare when you say

00:14:21.921 --> 00:14:25.985
you take the closest
one, for example.

00:14:25.986 --> 00:14:30.845
This already lowers
as compared to what?

00:14:30.846 --> 00:14:33.670
&gt;&gt; This comparison is done,

00:14:33.671 --> 00:14:35.840
there's a simulation where we're

00:14:35.841 --> 00:14:39.250
comparing a call system,
in other words,

00:14:39.251 --> 00:14:41.105
a ride-hailing style system

00:14:41.106 --> 00:14:43.720
with a street-hailing service,

00:14:43.721 --> 00:14:46.170
like a taxi service.

00:14:46.171 --> 00:14:49.675
&gt;&gt; How did you get
that information from taxi?

00:14:49.676 --> 00:14:51.960
&gt;&gt; This is a simulation again.

00:14:51.961 --> 00:14:56.560
There's a grid, and on
the x-axis is congestion level,

00:14:56.561 --> 00:14:58.630
meaning how many riders

00:14:58.631 --> 00:15:00.190
and drivers did we
simulate in the grid,

00:15:00.191 --> 00:15:02.090
what's the density of
riders and drivers.

00:15:02.091 --> 00:15:12.770
This is not based on actual data.

00:15:12.771 --> 00:15:15.390
So, I'll show you a mechanism

00:15:15.391 --> 00:15:17.490
that can further reduce
these pickup times,

00:15:17.491 --> 00:15:18.590
it's called Trip Upgrade,

00:15:18.591 --> 00:15:20.025
and it's a simple idea.

00:15:20.026 --> 00:15:23.820
So, if a rider is matched
with a driver and

00:15:23.821 --> 00:15:25.090
then another driver becomes

00:15:25.091 --> 00:15:27.460
available and another
rider requests a ride,

00:15:27.461 --> 00:15:30.230
it may be advantageous
to all parties if we

00:15:30.231 --> 00:15:33.870
swap the match instead of
matching rider B with driver B.

00:15:33.871 --> 00:15:36.670
So, simple concept,
this match swapping,

00:15:36.671 --> 00:15:41.300
but again, it has to make
everybody's pickup time lower.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.545
So, here's an illustration,

00:15:44.546 --> 00:15:47.780
so we'll start from
the beginning.

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:55.340
So, there's rider A and

00:15:55.341 --> 00:15:56.960
driver A, they're
matched together,

00:15:56.961 --> 00:15:59.370
they have a six-minute ETA,

00:15:59.371 --> 00:16:03.410
and then a second driver
and a second rider appear,

00:16:03.411 --> 00:16:05.455
and the match is swapped.

00:16:05.456 --> 00:16:08.260
So, simple mechanism,
but you can see that it

00:16:08.261 --> 00:16:11.360
decreases the pickup time
for all the parties.

00:16:11.361 --> 00:16:13.980
There's a variety of
different mechanisms like this,

00:16:13.981 --> 00:16:16.350
I'm not going to get into
detail on any others because I

00:16:16.351 --> 00:16:19.740
want to dive deep in
the dynamic pricing context.

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:23.190
We will come back to
matching and tie it

00:16:23.191 --> 00:16:26.230
into dynamic pricing
in the last section.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.520
So, let's think
about how one might

00:16:30.521 --> 00:16:33.960
calculate a surge multiple.

00:16:33.961 --> 00:16:37.300
So, somehow this should be

00:16:37.301 --> 00:16:40.985
based on local supply and
demand conditions.

00:16:40.986 --> 00:16:44.290
We're somehow trying to
calibrate demand with supply.

00:16:44.291 --> 00:16:45.410
So, on the left hand side, I'm

00:16:45.411 --> 00:16:49.310
showing a key measure of demand,

00:16:49.311 --> 00:16:51.760
which is how many riders are
opening the app and checking

00:16:51.761 --> 00:16:55.935
the price as a function
of geolocation.

00:16:55.936 --> 00:16:58.070
On the bottom left, I'm showing

00:16:58.071 --> 00:17:00.550
the number of open vehicles,

00:17:00.551 --> 00:17:03.240
vehicles that are
available to dispatch.

00:17:03.241 --> 00:17:05.770
Somehow, we need to combine
these together with

00:17:05.771 --> 00:17:07.530
some other inputs to

00:17:07.531 --> 00:17:08.645
calculate a surge multiple.

00:17:08.646 --> 00:17:09.710
Was there a question in the back?

00:17:09.711 --> 00:17:11.860
&gt;&gt; Is the surge multiple
expected not only to

00:17:11.861 --> 00:17:13.930
reduce the demand but
also increase the supply?

00:17:13.931 --> 00:17:16.010
Is it predictable so

00:17:16.011 --> 00:17:18.685
that drivers will start
driving when there's a surge?

00:17:18.686 --> 00:17:21.360
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:17:21.361 --> 00:17:23.290
What tends to happen
is that drivers

00:17:23.291 --> 00:17:25.240
know the time periods
and locations in

00:17:25.241 --> 00:17:28.530
which there tends to be
high surge and you do see

00:17:28.531 --> 00:17:32.960
that they they come
out and drive more in,

00:17:32.961 --> 00:17:35.520
at bar close for example,

00:17:35.521 --> 00:17:38.820
or in morning or
evening rush hour,

00:17:38.821 --> 00:17:40.750
these peak times
and locations. Yes.

00:17:40.751 --> 00:17:43.010
&gt;&gt; It's an effect that
you'd have to see over

00:17:43.011 --> 00:17:45.580
time as people learn the
surge multiple right?

00:17:45.581 --> 00:17:48.580
&gt;&gt; That's correct.

00:17:48.581 --> 00:17:50.240
There's more debate
about whether they

00:17:50.241 --> 00:17:52.705
respond to the surge in
a very short-term way,

00:17:52.706 --> 00:17:53.900
like when they see the surge,

00:17:53.901 --> 00:17:55.420
they can see the surge heatmap.

00:17:55.421 --> 00:17:58.530
But, there is a lot of evidence
that they do come out and

00:17:58.531 --> 00:17:59.930
drive more at the times and

00:17:59.931 --> 00:18:02.205
locations where there
tends to be surge.

00:18:02.206 --> 00:18:10.395
How might we determine
this surge multiple?

00:18:10.396 --> 00:18:13.750
So, I'm going to go back to

00:18:13.751 --> 00:18:17.190
very simple concepts
from your Econ101 class.

00:18:17.191 --> 00:18:18.600
We're going to build on these,

00:18:18.601 --> 00:18:22.110
this kind of model doesn't
work straight out of the box,

00:18:22.111 --> 00:18:23.590
so you could imagine there's

00:18:23.591 --> 00:18:26.040
a demand curve as a
function of price,

00:18:26.041 --> 00:18:28.250
there's a supply curve
as a function of price,

00:18:28.251 --> 00:18:30.500
and there's a market
equilibrium price

00:18:30.501 --> 00:18:31.910
where those two intersect here,

00:18:31.911 --> 00:18:33.380
I'm just neglecting the fact that

00:18:33.381 --> 00:18:35.855
the platform takes
a commission for simplicity,

00:18:35.856 --> 00:18:38.910
but it doesn't fundamentally
change the spectrum.

00:18:38.911 --> 00:18:41.300
So, we'll use the concept of

00:18:41.301 --> 00:18:43.720
producer surplus and
consumer surplus;

00:18:43.721 --> 00:18:45.240
the producer in our case being

00:18:45.241 --> 00:18:49.205
the driver and the consumer
being the rider,

00:18:49.206 --> 00:18:52.630
and so this area in blue

00:18:52.631 --> 00:18:56.490
is a measure of how much value
is created for the driver,

00:18:56.491 --> 00:18:57.900
the difference between
the amount that the

00:18:57.901 --> 00:19:00.260
driver's paid and basically,

00:19:00.261 --> 00:19:03.755
their costs including
opportunity costs.

00:19:03.756 --> 00:19:06.390
On the red area is a measure of

00:19:06.391 --> 00:19:10.255
how much rider value is
created on the platform,

00:19:10.256 --> 00:19:13.230
in other words, the difference
between the value that

00:19:13.231 --> 00:19:14.560
the rider gains from

00:19:14.561 --> 00:19:16.605
the ride and the amount
that they're paying.

00:19:16.606 --> 00:19:19.550
So, if we add up the red area
and the blue area,

00:19:19.551 --> 00:19:20.920
that's called the welfare,

00:19:20.921 --> 00:19:22.470
and that's a measure of

00:19:22.471 --> 00:19:25.455
how much value was created
for riders and drivers.

00:19:25.456 --> 00:19:30.110
So, it's not this simple in
the context of ride hailing,

00:19:30.111 --> 00:19:32.480
but we need these concepts,

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:36.720
and notice that this market
equilibrium price

00:19:36.721 --> 00:19:41.210
is the price that
maximizes the welfare.

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:44.800
So, in our context,

00:19:44.801 --> 00:19:47.150
there's three states that
drivers cycle through,

00:19:47.151 --> 00:19:49.100
Michael referred to this earlier.

00:19:49.101 --> 00:19:50.810
There's the open state
when they're

00:19:50.811 --> 00:19:52.230
available to be dispatched,

00:19:52.231 --> 00:19:53.860
there's the enroute state
where they're on

00:19:53.861 --> 00:19:55.800
their way to pick up a rider,

00:19:55.801 --> 00:19:57.870
and there's the on-trip state.

00:19:57.871 --> 00:20:02.300
So, the riders are
sensitive not just

00:20:02.301 --> 00:20:04.460
to price but also to pickup

00:20:04.461 --> 00:20:07.150
time because that's
the wasted time for them.

00:20:07.151 --> 00:20:09.560
The drivers on the other hand
are sensitive to

00:20:09.561 --> 00:20:12.190
both open and enroute time

00:20:12.191 --> 00:20:14.220
because they're not being
paid during that time period;

00:20:14.221 --> 00:20:16.070
they're paid during this
on-trip time period.

00:20:16.071 --> 00:20:21.500
&gt;&gt; I don't think

00:20:21.501 --> 00:20:23.240
it will give them [inaudible]

00:20:23.241 --> 00:20:25.760
open time into
two different types of time,

00:20:25.761 --> 00:20:26.915
moving and not moving,

00:20:26.916 --> 00:20:28.160
like rebalancing and
not rebalancing.

00:20:28.161 --> 00:20:28.980
&gt;&gt; Rebalancing and
not rebalancing.

00:20:28.981 --> 00:20:30.645
&gt;&gt; So, how much of rebalancing

00:20:30.646 --> 00:20:32.450
happens during that open time,

00:20:32.451 --> 00:20:37.670
like, do they move or do they stand
still like, roughly [inaudible]

00:20:37.671 --> 00:20:38.695
&gt;&gt; That's a great question,

00:20:38.696 --> 00:20:40.110
so how much rebalancing occurs?

00:20:40.111 --> 00:20:41.830
There is a fair amount
of rebalancing

00:20:41.831 --> 00:20:43.685
that occurs in these systems.

00:20:43.686 --> 00:20:45.890
Sometimes the drivers do it
while they're online and

00:20:45.891 --> 00:20:49.370
sometimes they go
offline and move.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.540
So, we have some
visibility into that

00:20:53.541 --> 00:20:57.420
but it's also a

00:20:57.421 --> 00:20:58.570
little bit tricky to measure how

00:20:58.571 --> 00:21:00.670
much rebalancing
time, interestingly.

00:21:00.671 --> 00:21:03.425
Because if they go
out of the app,

00:21:03.426 --> 00:21:04.770
turn off the app,

00:21:04.771 --> 00:21:06.965
and then come back on in
a different rotation-

00:21:06.966 --> 00:21:08.520
&gt;&gt; Is there a real
strategy for them

00:21:08.521 --> 00:21:11.370
to kill the app on
when they rebalance?

00:21:11.371 --> 00:21:13.750
&gt;&gt; Not necessarily,
interestingly.

00:21:13.751 --> 00:21:16.095
Because sometimes they will,

00:21:16.096 --> 00:21:18.310
for example, in San Francisco,

00:21:18.311 --> 00:21:20.480
they drive all the way
out into the suburbs,

00:21:20.481 --> 00:21:22.120
they often want to rebalance

00:21:22.121 --> 00:21:25.060
drive back into the city
to get more rides,

00:21:25.061 --> 00:21:26.400
but if they leave their app on

00:21:26.401 --> 00:21:27.470
while they're driving
up the highway,

00:21:27.471 --> 00:21:29.600
they can be dispatched
all along the way,

00:21:29.601 --> 00:21:32.975
and the drivers report that they

00:21:32.976 --> 00:21:34.540
do better in terms of earnings if

00:21:34.541 --> 00:21:36.305
they rebalance all the way
back to the city.

00:21:36.306 --> 00:21:40.320
So, which is they do sometimes
go out of the app to move,

00:21:40.321 --> 00:21:42.830
to change geographic
location and response to

00:21:42.831 --> 00:21:45.450
perceived earnings difference
by geolocation.

00:21:45.451 --> 00:21:47.765
&gt;&gt; Quick question about
that sort of stuff.

00:21:47.766 --> 00:21:53.030
Do you have evidence of people
that are using both Lyft,

00:21:53.031 --> 00:21:57.005
truck drivers that are Lyft
and Uber, and if you do,

00:21:57.006 --> 00:21:59.520
is there's a competitive pressure

00:21:59.521 --> 00:22:01.480
on for providing
a better experience

00:22:01.481 --> 00:22:03.580
that it was the economics
here in terms

00:22:03.581 --> 00:22:07.180
of competing for
the attention of the drivers?

00:22:07.181 --> 00:22:08.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah. The competitive angle

00:22:08.881 --> 00:22:10.345
is obviously an important one,

00:22:10.346 --> 00:22:11.845
it's a pretty complicated one.

00:22:11.846 --> 00:22:12.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:12.101 --> 00:22:13.180
&gt;&gt; So, I think we're
going to leave it

00:22:13.181 --> 00:22:14.625
aside for the purposes
of this talk.

00:22:14.626 --> 00:22:16.060
But yes, there's definitely,

00:22:16.061 --> 00:22:17.990
is not this simple because in

00:22:17.991 --> 00:22:20.360
fact one of the model
that I'll introduce,

00:22:20.361 --> 00:22:23.950
the simplified model
assumes that the number of

00:22:23.951 --> 00:22:25.670
drivers that are on the
system as a response

00:22:25.671 --> 00:22:27.860
just to the price
and the earnings,

00:22:27.861 --> 00:22:29.590
and that there's no
competitor in that picture,

00:22:29.591 --> 00:22:30.640
it's a closed system,

00:22:30.641 --> 00:22:32.620
there's only one
geographic region,

00:22:32.621 --> 00:22:34.380
so it's simplified
in a lot of ways and

00:22:34.381 --> 00:22:36.960
the lack of a competitor

00:22:36.961 --> 00:22:38.860
in the model that I'll show

00:22:38.861 --> 00:22:41.360
is one of the simplifications.

00:22:41.361 --> 00:22:43.080
&gt;&gt; I was just imagining
to decide at this,

00:22:43.081 --> 00:22:45.930
you probably have very
strong evidence by launching

00:22:45.931 --> 00:22:48.030
these drivers jumped around town

00:22:48.031 --> 00:22:50.680
[inaudible] online that their
taxi using these services,

00:22:50.681 --> 00:22:52.140
they're actually
[inaudible] to services.

00:22:52.141 --> 00:22:54.310
&gt;&gt; It can be hard to tell
if the driver turned

00:22:54.311 --> 00:22:56.825
off and got went out of
app in order to move,

00:22:56.826 --> 00:22:58.560
rebalance, or took a trip.

00:22:58.561 --> 00:22:59.960
On competitor, for example,

00:22:59.961 --> 00:23:01.895
there's a limited visibility
into that, yeah.

00:23:01.896 --> 00:23:05.935
&gt;&gt; So, like in
my [inaudible] conjecture

00:23:05.936 --> 00:23:07.560
that I thought was obvious but

00:23:07.561 --> 00:23:10.480
maybe it's false that if

00:23:10.481 --> 00:23:14.375
the matching pricing
efficiently design,

00:23:14.376 --> 00:23:18.650
then it would never be
individually optimal

00:23:18.651 --> 00:23:23.715
for a driver to switch off
the app while he rebalances.

00:23:23.716 --> 00:23:26.890
If the matching price
does efficiently,

00:23:26.891 --> 00:23:29.810
I would have sold that
he would always be

00:23:29.811 --> 00:23:33.660
individually better off
by actually taking three,

00:23:33.661 --> 00:23:36.495
rather than giving
up that opportunity.

00:23:36.496 --> 00:23:39.075
Is that theorem true?

00:23:39.076 --> 00:23:44.360
In practice, the drivers
report that they sometimes-

00:23:44.361 --> 00:23:45.845
&gt;&gt; Let might simpler if that

00:23:45.846 --> 00:23:49.890
the matching pricing that
you use is not efficient.

00:23:49.891 --> 00:23:52.520
So, is the kind of driver that

00:23:52.521 --> 00:23:55.910
reported the proof that the
system was inefficient,

00:23:55.911 --> 00:23:57.230
or is might conjectural?

00:23:57.231 --> 00:24:02.765
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I think it's a
reasonable to assume that

00:24:02.766 --> 00:24:04.760
these matching and
pricing algorithms

00:24:04.761 --> 00:24:06.330
are not perfectly efficient

00:24:06.331 --> 00:24:10.900
in terms of being incentive
compatible and so on.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.340
Okay. So, there's the sense of

00:24:17.341 --> 00:24:19.570
sensitivity to pickup time
in addition to

00:24:19.571 --> 00:24:25.600
price and to the welfare
maximizing price.

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:28.450
There's actually a market
equilibrium which is

00:24:28.451 --> 00:24:30.580
a price and pickup time that

00:24:30.581 --> 00:24:32.400
maximizes the welfare for which

00:24:32.401 --> 00:24:34.520
there's not much wasted time
in the system.

00:24:34.521 --> 00:24:36.110
So, on the left hand side,

00:24:36.111 --> 00:24:37.680
I'm showing a situation

00:24:37.681 --> 00:24:38.920
where there's not
very many riders and

00:24:38.921 --> 00:24:40.870
not very many drivers
unless you can imagine

00:24:40.871 --> 00:24:43.850
the pickup times in
this scenario very long,

00:24:43.851 --> 00:24:46.440
and so riders and drivers engage

00:24:46.441 --> 00:24:48.250
at lower rates with the platform.

00:24:48.251 --> 00:24:49.700
Whereas on the right hand side,

00:24:49.701 --> 00:24:52.504
with more riders and
drivers in the system,

00:24:52.505 --> 00:24:54.305
the pickup times are low,

00:24:54.306 --> 00:24:56.910
and the engagement is high.

00:24:56.911 --> 00:24:59.165
So, you'll see that

00:24:59.166 --> 00:25:01.810
the welfare-maximizing
price and pick

00:25:01.811 --> 00:25:05.290
up time combination looks
like this right hand side.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:11.515
Let's make that concept rigorous.

00:25:11.516 --> 00:25:13.710
One key aspect is that there's

00:25:13.711 --> 00:25:17.430
a very strong mathematical
relationship between number of

00:25:17.431 --> 00:25:19.830
open cars in a particular
geographic region

00:25:19.831 --> 00:25:23.060
and the pickup time.

00:25:23.061 --> 00:25:25.290
So here what I'm showing
you is we've picked

00:25:25.291 --> 00:25:26.920
a particular
geographic region and

00:25:26.921 --> 00:25:28.925
we've looked at
different time periods,

00:25:28.926 --> 00:25:31.250
and we've gathered data on

00:25:31.251 --> 00:25:33.865
the average pickup time
and on the number of

00:25:33.866 --> 00:25:36.755
open cars during
those time periods.

00:25:36.756 --> 00:25:39.040
Then for each particular
value of the open cars,

00:25:39.041 --> 00:25:40.590
like 40 open cars,

00:25:40.591 --> 00:25:43.030
all time periods in which
there were 40 open cars,

00:25:43.031 --> 00:25:45.465
we looked at what was
the average pickup time,

00:25:45.466 --> 00:25:47.900
and that's what's shown
in the blue curve.

00:25:47.901 --> 00:25:51.140
You can see that the pickup time

00:25:51.141 --> 00:25:54.070
decreases as a function of
the number of open cars,

00:25:54.071 --> 00:25:56.820
and in fact, it's
fit remarkably well

00:25:56.821 --> 00:26:00.535
with a log linear model
shown in red.

00:26:00.536 --> 00:26:02.350
So, this is clear

00:26:02.351 --> 00:26:04.340
mathematical relationship
between the two.

00:26:04.341 --> 00:26:07.815
Another piece that we'll need is

00:26:07.816 --> 00:26:12.855
a steady-state formula for
the number of cars on-trip.

00:26:12.856 --> 00:26:16.715
So, if we look at the
trip completion rate capital C

00:26:16.716 --> 00:26:19.425
and we multiply by
the amount of time,

00:26:19.426 --> 00:26:21.320
the average length of a trip,

00:26:21.321 --> 00:26:23.490
the average duration
of the trip that gives

00:26:23.491 --> 00:26:25.865
us the steady-state
number of cars on-trip.

00:26:25.866 --> 00:26:28.860
So, this is an application
of Little's Law.

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:31.850
So, what we're going to do

00:26:31.851 --> 00:26:33.755
in the next slide
is we're going to

00:26:33.756 --> 00:26:35.780
break down the number of

00:26:35.781 --> 00:26:38.030
total cars by the number
of cars that are on-trip,

00:26:38.031 --> 00:26:39.545
the number of cars that are open,

00:26:39.546 --> 00:26:42.725
and the number of cars
that are enroute.

00:26:42.726 --> 00:26:46.225
So here you go, the three states.

00:26:46.226 --> 00:26:49.060
Right now, let's take
a fixed number of total cars.

00:26:49.061 --> 00:26:52.830
In fact, in later
slides a note that

00:26:52.831 --> 00:26:55.880
the total number of cars

00:26:55.881 --> 00:26:57.210
is actually a function of

00:26:57.211 --> 00:26:59.080
the price and the pickup
time and so on.

00:26:59.081 --> 00:27:00.830
But for right now, just think

00:27:00.831 --> 00:27:02.760
about capital N as being fixed,

00:27:02.761 --> 00:27:05.420
and then decompose the number

00:27:05.421 --> 00:27:08.480
of cars into the number
of open cars capital O,

00:27:08.481 --> 00:27:10.510
the number of on-trip
cars which is

00:27:10.511 --> 00:27:13.155
the product of
this trip completion rates,

00:27:13.156 --> 00:27:17.020
and the trip time capital T,

00:27:17.021 --> 00:27:18.900
and then let's apply
Little's Law again on

00:27:18.901 --> 00:27:20.870
the right-hand side to
calculate the number of

00:27:20.871 --> 00:27:24.745
cars that are in this on
route or pickup state.

00:27:24.746 --> 00:27:28.305
So, that's the
trip completion rate

00:27:28.306 --> 00:27:31.980
times this at pickup time, ETA,

00:27:31.981 --> 00:27:34.790
from two slides
ago that function,

00:27:34.791 --> 00:27:37.250
decreasing function as a
function of the number

00:27:37.251 --> 00:27:40.250
of open cars. Yes?

00:27:40.251 --> 00:27:43.255
&gt;&gt; So, trip completion
rate definition?

00:27:43.256 --> 00:27:45.640
&gt;&gt; Okay. So what
we're going to see,

00:27:45.641 --> 00:27:47.750
this is a mathematical
formula for how

00:27:47.751 --> 00:27:52.100
the trip completion rate must
relate to these numbers.

00:27:52.101 --> 00:27:53.285
But we'll see in a moment,

00:27:53.286 --> 00:27:55.910
we're going to use this
in order to calculate

00:27:55.911 --> 00:27:57.080
the trip completion rate as

00:27:57.081 --> 00:27:59.405
a function of the number
of total cars and so on.

00:27:59.406 --> 00:28:01.805
Just the number of trips
completed per unit time.

00:28:01.806 --> 00:28:05.470
&gt;&gt; Are trips completed per time?

00:28:05.471 --> 00:28:06.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:06.681 --> 00:28:10.300
Is that clear?

00:28:10.301 --> 00:28:11.400
In steady-state the number

00:28:11.401 --> 00:28:12.570
of trips completed for open time.

00:28:12.571 --> 00:28:14.745
So, it's like the throughput
of the system,

00:28:14.746 --> 00:28:19.505
how many trips are we creating
with the system. Yeah?

00:28:19.506 --> 00:28:21.885
&gt;&gt; I wonder if
you've thought about

00:28:21.886 --> 00:28:25.010
the obvious four stage
of a driver,

00:28:25.011 --> 00:28:28.340
which is I am not
logged into the app?

00:28:28.341 --> 00:28:28.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:28.611 --> 00:28:31.010
&gt;&gt; But hey, I would have
to get a ping if there's

00:28:31.011 --> 00:28:33.815
a search happening,
so it'll get open?

00:28:33.816 --> 00:28:34.115
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:34.116 --> 00:28:38.450
&gt;&gt; Because people take lunches,

00:28:38.451 --> 00:28:40.460
or they at home
watching the game,

00:28:40.461 --> 00:28:43.460
or they decide what is
a good time to make some money.

00:28:43.461 --> 00:28:44.210
&gt;&gt; Okay?

00:28:44.211 --> 00:28:45.380
&gt;&gt; So, I wonder if that will

00:28:45.381 --> 00:28:47.865
affect your modeling in anyway.

00:28:47.866 --> 00:28:49.880
&gt;&gt; Well, certainly
there's a sense in

00:28:49.881 --> 00:28:52.310
which drivers over
the long term will

00:28:52.311 --> 00:28:54.640
respond to the amount of money
that they're earning and

00:28:54.641 --> 00:28:55.650
that's what we built into

00:28:55.651 --> 00:28:57.665
the model a couple
of slides later.

00:28:57.666 --> 00:29:01.090
You're talking about
a shorter term aspect,

00:29:01.091 --> 00:29:02.830
which is if they're
search pricing right now,

00:29:02.831 --> 00:29:05.240
maybe we should give
a push notification.

00:29:05.241 --> 00:29:06.230
&gt;&gt; But there's a fourth state.

00:29:06.231 --> 00:29:08.815
&gt;&gt; Yeah, there is a fourth
state. So it turns out that

00:29:08.816 --> 00:29:11.560
you can capture
that fourth state through

00:29:11.561 --> 00:29:13.950
modeling in capital N.
Instead of thinking about

00:29:13.951 --> 00:29:17.350
capital N the number of
cars as being fixed,

00:29:17.351 --> 00:29:18.670
what we're going to do is
we're going to model it

00:29:18.671 --> 00:29:20.475
as a function of the earnings,

00:29:20.476 --> 00:29:22.560
and that's implicitly taking into

00:29:22.561 --> 00:29:24.810
account the fact that
drivers have another option,

00:29:24.811 --> 00:29:26.700
which is not to drive at all.

00:29:26.701 --> 00:29:28.725
Good question. Yeah?

00:29:28.726 --> 00:29:30.655
&gt;&gt; Congestion level
doesn't affect

00:29:30.656 --> 00:29:33.020
the pickup time level
and car relationship?

00:29:33.021 --> 00:29:34.630
&gt;&gt; Congestion level, traffic,

00:29:34.631 --> 00:29:35.670
and so on, certainly,

00:29:35.671 --> 00:29:40.890
it does affect the pickup time

00:29:40.891 --> 00:29:43.370
and the trip duration capital

00:29:43.371 --> 00:29:47.270
T. We're not going to
focus on that aspect.

00:29:47.271 --> 00:29:49.470
The model that we'll

00:29:49.471 --> 00:29:51.720
use will be focused on
a particular time of week.

00:29:51.721 --> 00:29:53.700
So, we'll condition
on time of week,

00:29:53.701 --> 00:29:54.900
and if you say take

00:29:54.901 --> 00:29:56.935
downtown San Francisco
in morning rush hour,

00:29:56.936 --> 00:29:58.940
traffic conditions are roughly

00:29:58.941 --> 00:30:00.970
consistent during
that time period,

00:30:00.971 --> 00:30:02.440
and so then we'll just assume

00:30:02.441 --> 00:30:05.485
a background traffic level
as we fit these models.

00:30:05.486 --> 00:30:07.000
For example, as we are fitting

00:30:07.001 --> 00:30:11.810
that curve for pickup time
versus number of open cars,

00:30:11.811 --> 00:30:13.220
that should be fit just for

00:30:13.221 --> 00:30:14.960
morning rush hour in

00:30:14.961 --> 00:30:17.020
that particular geographic
area, for example.

00:30:17.021 --> 00:30:22.175
That's a great question. Yes.

00:30:22.176 --> 00:30:26.805
&gt;&gt; When you fit
the average pickup time model,

00:30:26.806 --> 00:30:27.920
what was the source of

00:30:27.921 --> 00:30:30.645
the variance in the
number of open cars?

00:30:30.646 --> 00:30:34.320
So, was it because
there's a rush hour.

00:30:34.321 --> 00:30:39.230
&gt;&gt; Sure, there's variability

00:30:39.231 --> 00:30:42.420
in the number of open cars
simply because, first of all,

00:30:42.421 --> 00:30:45.460
if we assume that the
drivers are arriving

00:30:45.461 --> 00:30:46.990
according to a Poisson process

00:30:46.991 --> 00:30:48.900
and we have a fixed
geographic region,

00:30:48.901 --> 00:30:51.040
the number of cars that

00:30:51.041 --> 00:30:53.685
arrive is a Poisson
random variable.

00:30:53.686 --> 00:30:56.540
So, there will naturally
be some variability,

00:30:56.541 --> 00:30:57.590
particularly if we look at a

00:30:57.591 --> 00:30:59.070
relatively small
geographic region,

00:30:59.071 --> 00:31:00.900
there's some natural variability
in the number of

00:31:00.901 --> 00:31:05.170
open cars just due to
that Poisson noise, for example.

00:31:05.171 --> 00:31:07.870
Then there's a variety of
additional factors which lead

00:31:07.871 --> 00:31:10.710
to additional noise in
the number of open cars.

00:31:10.711 --> 00:31:12.930
&gt;&gt; But you'd have to
control for things

00:31:12.931 --> 00:31:15.210
like some street was

00:31:15.211 --> 00:31:19.740
shut down or stuck
in traffic though.

00:31:19.741 --> 00:31:20.975
&gt;&gt; In the model for

00:31:20.976 --> 00:31:24.045
pickup time as a
function of open cars,

00:31:24.046 --> 00:31:26.670
this model didn't explicitly

00:31:26.671 --> 00:31:29.040
take any subtleties
like that into account.

00:31:29.041 --> 00:31:31.050
It's just a simple relationship
between the two.

00:31:31.051 --> 00:31:35.575
It's a surprisingly good
relationship in this case.

00:31:35.576 --> 00:31:37.580
&gt;&gt; To me, it seems
surprisingly wide

00:31:37.581 --> 00:31:39.355
variance in the number.

00:31:39.356 --> 00:31:40.750
&gt;&gt; The number of open cars.

00:31:40.751 --> 00:31:44.185
&gt;&gt; It went from
literally zeros to.

00:31:44.186 --> 00:31:46.060
&gt;&gt; Well, there's not
very many time periods in

00:31:46.061 --> 00:31:48.390
which we saw those very
high numbers of open cars,

00:31:48.391 --> 00:31:49.210
which is why it was a little

00:31:49.211 --> 00:31:51.440
noisier down on
the right hand side.

00:31:52.500 --> 00:31:54.790
&gt;&gt; So, the number of open cars,

00:31:54.791 --> 00:31:56.185
that's an actual number,

00:31:56.186 --> 00:31:58.660
like 36 means
36 open cars, right?

00:31:58.661 --> 00:31:58.975
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:58.976 --> 00:32:01.395
&gt;&gt; So, roughly what percentage

00:32:01.396 --> 00:32:04.080
of the entire fleet
does it represent?

00:32:04.081 --> 00:32:06.490
How big is that geo?

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:09.610
&gt;&gt; Okay, how big is
the geo that this is based

00:32:09.611 --> 00:32:12.000
on? That's a great question.

00:32:12.001 --> 00:32:16.040
I think this is roughly a
San Francisco downtown area

00:32:16.041 --> 00:32:17.865
but I don't know how
big exactly the geo is.

00:32:17.866 --> 00:32:21.190
&gt;&gt; So, roughly, what's
the total number of

00:32:21.191 --> 00:32:25.095
cars that are occupied?

00:32:25.096 --> 00:32:27.790
&gt;&gt; That are on trip and so on.

00:32:27.791 --> 00:32:29.010
In other words, how
does it roughly

00:32:29.011 --> 00:32:30.410
breakdown between
the three slides.

00:32:30.411 --> 00:32:31.790
&gt;&gt; Right. Percentage roughly,

00:32:31.791 --> 00:32:33.710
open cars versus
booked cars [inaudible].

00:32:33.711 --> 00:32:36.175
&gt;&gt; It's a great question.

00:32:36.176 --> 00:32:37.470
It depends on whether.

00:32:37.471 --> 00:32:38.520
&gt;&gt; On your previous slide,

00:32:38.521 --> 00:32:40.140
there was actually an answer,

00:32:40.141 --> 00:32:41.745
I just realized, it's 40.

00:32:41.746 --> 00:32:44.720
Wait, that couldn't be right,

00:32:44.840 --> 00:32:48.000
because you said
about half the time,

00:32:48.001 --> 00:32:50.250
cars are on trip.

00:32:50.251 --> 00:32:51.360
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's correct.

00:32:51.361 --> 00:32:52.820
&gt;&gt; Which means that
half the time,

00:32:52.821 --> 00:32:56.615
they're either open or
going to the passenger.

00:32:56.616 --> 00:32:58.360
&gt;&gt; As a first-order processes?

00:32:58.361 --> 00:33:00.140
&gt;&gt; So, let's say half
of them, they're open.

00:33:00.141 --> 00:33:03.170
So, that sounds
strange that there are

00:33:03.171 --> 00:33:06.510
so few cars in the entire
San Francisco area.

00:33:06.511 --> 00:33:09.015
There are only, most of the time.

00:33:09.016 --> 00:33:10.620
&gt;&gt; It's not the whole
San Francisco area,

00:33:10.621 --> 00:33:11.740
it's some sub region

00:33:11.741 --> 00:33:13.055
of downtown but I
don't know how big.

00:33:13.056 --> 00:33:14.000
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:33:14.001 --> 00:33:19.760
&gt;&gt; Good. So, let's
take that supply model

00:33:19.761 --> 00:33:22.630
from this slide and

00:33:22.631 --> 00:33:25.070
let's rearrange to put
trip completion rate,

00:33:25.071 --> 00:33:28.200
which is a measure
of value created,

00:33:28.201 --> 00:33:29.625
the number of trips created,

00:33:29.626 --> 00:33:31.505
let's put that on
the left-hand side.

00:33:31.506 --> 00:33:33.575
On the right-hand side,

00:33:33.576 --> 00:33:36.830
the number of open cars
and this pickup time,

00:33:36.831 --> 00:33:39.450
which is also a function of
the number of open cars.

00:33:39.451 --> 00:33:41.060
Let's just treat N and T

00:33:41.061 --> 00:33:42.410
as being fixed for
the moment but, again,

00:33:42.411 --> 00:33:45.240
we'll come back and
correct that in a minute.

00:33:45.241 --> 00:33:48.630
So, if we look at the
trip completion rate, capital C,

00:33:48.631 --> 00:33:50.180
as a function of the
number of open cars,

00:33:50.181 --> 00:33:51.500
that's the red curve,

00:33:51.501 --> 00:33:53.230
and then the blue curve is
just the curve that you

00:33:53.231 --> 00:33:55.280
looked at before,
the pickup time,

00:33:55.281 --> 00:33:56.880
and you can see that

00:33:56.881 --> 00:33:58.855
the trip completion rate
is non-monotonic,

00:33:58.856 --> 00:34:01.125
that there's an optimal value,

00:34:01.126 --> 00:34:03.400
O star, for the number of

00:34:03.401 --> 00:34:07.375
open cars to maximize the
trip completion rate.

00:34:07.376 --> 00:34:09.524
Now, if you go above,

00:34:09.525 --> 00:34:11.350
for values of the number of

00:34:11.351 --> 00:34:13.500
open cars that are above O star,

00:34:13.501 --> 00:34:15.930
there's a bit of
the trade off here.

00:34:15.931 --> 00:34:18.350
If the number of
open cars is higher,

00:34:18.351 --> 00:34:20.690
the trip completion rate
is slightly lower,

00:34:20.691 --> 00:34:22.370
but then the pickup time is also

00:34:22.371 --> 00:34:23.820
slightly lower so there might be

00:34:23.821 --> 00:34:27.360
an experience versus
throughput trade off here.

00:34:27.361 --> 00:34:30.150
So, O star is

00:34:30.151 --> 00:34:32.950
one way to think about
an optimal system but maybe

00:34:32.951 --> 00:34:35.370
there's a bit of
a trade off and there's

00:34:35.371 --> 00:34:36.600
a consideration around

00:34:36.601 --> 00:34:38.655
slightly higher levels
of open cars.

00:34:38.656 --> 00:34:40.530
On the other hand, if
you go to the left,

00:34:40.531 --> 00:34:43.240
there's really no advantage
at all, just disadvantage.

00:34:43.241 --> 00:34:45.410
So, we look at what
happens to the left,

00:34:45.411 --> 00:34:48.695
the number of trips
being completed drops,

00:34:48.696 --> 00:34:52.230
and additionally,
the pickup time increases.

00:34:52.231 --> 00:34:53.910
So, more wasted time
for riders and

00:34:53.911 --> 00:34:55.690
drivers, fewer trips completed.

00:34:55.691 --> 00:34:58.400
It's not advantageous to
anyone to be in this zone,

00:34:58.401 --> 00:35:00.675
and I'll call this
the danger zone.

00:35:00.676 --> 00:35:04.115
You'll see that it
has some implications

00:35:04.116 --> 00:35:07.620
for the ability of

00:35:07.621 --> 00:35:11.500
the system to serve
riders and drivers.

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:15.380
So, that was a model
that just involved

00:35:15.381 --> 00:35:19.775
the cars in
different states. Yes?

00:35:19.776 --> 00:35:22.120
&gt;&gt; The danger zone,

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.020
whats the implication
in the danger zone

00:35:25.021 --> 00:35:27.325
for the earnings per driver?

00:35:27.326 --> 00:35:30.600
Since you have fewer
open cars all the time,

00:35:30.601 --> 00:35:31.910
it would seem that

00:35:31.911 --> 00:35:34.080
the drivers would earn
more in the danger zone.

00:35:34.081 --> 00:35:36.700
&gt;&gt; They actually don't earn
more in the danger zone.

00:35:36.701 --> 00:35:37.770
Fewer trips are completed.

00:35:37.771 --> 00:35:39.625
They spend more time picking up

00:35:39.626 --> 00:35:42.330
then they save in
terms of, they, say,

00:35:42.331 --> 00:35:44.200
spend less time open but they

00:35:44.201 --> 00:35:46.600
spend much more time picking up,

00:35:46.601 --> 00:35:48.000
and they're only
paid when they're

00:35:48.001 --> 00:35:49.565
in their on trip status.

00:35:49.566 --> 00:35:52.600
So actually, this does
not increase earnings.

00:35:52.601 --> 00:35:55.255
Great.

00:35:55.256 --> 00:35:58.000
So, maybe let's keep

00:35:58.001 --> 00:35:58.770
moving a little bit

00:35:58.771 --> 00:36:00.415
and then we'll come
back to the questions.

00:36:00.416 --> 00:36:03.740
So, let's tie back to prices,

00:36:03.741 --> 00:36:06.920
and let's think about
in order to tie into

00:36:06.921 --> 00:36:10.575
prices and how surge
prices might work,

00:36:10.576 --> 00:36:14.705
let's think about modelling
demand and supply.

00:36:14.706 --> 00:36:17.180
For example, we need
to understand how

00:36:17.181 --> 00:36:19.940
the trip request volume
on the rider side might

00:36:19.941 --> 00:36:25.500
respond to that pickup time
or to the surge multiple.

00:36:25.501 --> 00:36:28.680
So, a simple model for that
could be the following,

00:36:28.681 --> 00:36:30.510
the number of requests
as a function of

00:36:30.511 --> 00:36:33.430
the price and the pickup time.

00:36:33.431 --> 00:36:36.210
As the product of the
number of riders who

00:36:36.211 --> 00:36:38.940
are opening the app and
checking the price, Lambda,

00:36:38.941 --> 00:36:44.600
the function for
the price response, R,

00:36:44.601 --> 00:36:47.110
and a function for
the ETA response,

00:36:47.111 --> 00:36:49.730
G. This is a simple model
in which we assume

00:36:49.731 --> 00:36:51.640
probabilistic
independence between the

00:36:51.641 --> 00:36:53.670
two for illustration.

00:36:53.671 --> 00:36:57.445
If we look at the data
on the bottom,

00:36:57.446 --> 00:37:00.860
the fit actually is
pretty good for these.

00:37:00.861 --> 00:37:02.150
So, here's the surge multiple on

00:37:02.151 --> 00:37:05.125
the X-axis and on the Y-axis,

00:37:05.126 --> 00:37:08.020
the probability of request.

00:37:08.021 --> 00:37:10.870
You can see that the blue curve
is the data at each of

00:37:10.871 --> 00:37:12.720
these surge multiples and

00:37:12.721 --> 00:37:14.380
the red curve is
the fitted model.

00:37:14.381 --> 00:37:16.615
So, we have a pretty
good estimate except

00:37:16.616 --> 00:37:19.705
some interesting
non-monotonicity is

00:37:19.706 --> 00:37:23.370
here around 10 minutes ETA.

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:26.110
So, that's the demand model.

00:37:26.111 --> 00:37:27.910
Now, let's put some of
the pieces together.

00:37:27.911 --> 00:37:32.810
So, in a steady state model,

00:37:32.811 --> 00:37:35.660
the number of riders
requesting rides should be

00:37:35.661 --> 00:37:37.010
equal to the number of trips

00:37:37.011 --> 00:37:38.985
that are completing
per unit time.

00:37:38.986 --> 00:37:42.070
So, that demand model
on the left hand side

00:37:42.071 --> 00:37:44.950
should equal the number
of trips completing,

00:37:44.951 --> 00:37:47.105
which we derived on
the right hand side.

00:37:47.106 --> 00:37:48.750
Additionally, I'm not going to go

00:37:48.751 --> 00:37:50.175
into detail on this piece,

00:37:50.176 --> 00:37:52.320
but the number of
open cars, capital N,

00:37:52.321 --> 00:37:55.575
actually depends on how much
the drivers are earning.

00:37:55.576 --> 00:37:57.480
This is what we
talked about before.

00:37:57.481 --> 00:38:00.260
So, that's a function
of the price, P,

00:38:00.261 --> 00:38:02.680
is also a function of the
number of trips that are

00:38:02.681 --> 00:38:06.560
completing per
unit time per driver.

00:38:06.561 --> 00:38:09.580
So, solving this system
of equations yields

00:38:09.581 --> 00:38:12.130
the pickup time and

00:38:12.131 --> 00:38:14.210
the trip completion rate
that's associated

00:38:14.211 --> 00:38:16.290
with any given price level,

00:38:16.291 --> 00:38:18.530
P. On the next slide,

00:38:18.531 --> 00:38:23.010
we'll look at that,
the amount of value,

00:38:23.011 --> 00:38:27.135
the welfare created
at each price point,

00:38:27.136 --> 00:38:30.290
little p. This is, by the way,

00:38:30.291 --> 00:38:32.460
all described in
a paper by Castillo and

00:38:32.461 --> 00:38:33.510
colleagues and I'm not

00:38:33.511 --> 00:38:35.200
describing Uber's
surge pricing system,

00:38:35.201 --> 00:38:36.995
I'm describing how surge pricing

00:38:36.996 --> 00:38:40.080
can be done and the
concepts behind it.

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:46.315
So, let's look as a function
of the surge multiplier,

00:38:46.316 --> 00:38:48.640
at the amount of welfare
created in system,

00:38:48.641 --> 00:38:50.370
that's the red curve.

00:38:50.371 --> 00:38:55.900
I've also shown the curve
for the platform revenue and

00:38:55.901 --> 00:38:59.970
you can see that

00:38:59.971 --> 00:39:02.820
when the search multiple
drops below a certain level,

00:39:02.821 --> 00:39:04.680
the welfare drops off a cliff and

00:39:04.681 --> 00:39:07.300
much less welfare is created.

00:39:07.301 --> 00:39:09.605
The reason for that is that
this is the danger zone,

00:39:09.606 --> 00:39:10.995
this is the zone in which

00:39:10.996 --> 00:39:12.995
because these pickup
times get long,

00:39:12.996 --> 00:39:15.410
a lot of the supply is wasted in

00:39:15.411 --> 00:39:18.365
pickup time and it's much
more less efficient.

00:39:18.366 --> 00:39:21.400
So, the welfare maximizing prices

00:39:21.401 --> 00:39:25.940
basically just above that
cut off for the danger zone.

00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:33.000
If the price drops
below that threshold,

00:39:33.001 --> 00:39:34.860
the welfare drops precipitously.

00:39:34.861 --> 00:39:36.515
These pickup times go up,

00:39:36.516 --> 00:39:38.140
the time on trip drops,

00:39:38.141 --> 00:39:39.430
the trip completion rate drops.

00:39:39.431 --> 00:39:42.840
Okay, so what happens?

00:39:42.841 --> 00:39:44.280
The supply and demand conditions

00:39:44.281 --> 00:39:46.670
fluctuate over the course
of a day, and so in red,

00:39:46.671 --> 00:39:50.665
I'm showing a peak-hours again
in downtown San Francisco,

00:39:50.666 --> 00:39:53.045
and in blue, I'm showing
the non-peak hours

00:39:53.046 --> 00:39:54.550
in downtown San Francisco.

00:39:54.551 --> 00:39:57.645
You can see that the
location of this danger zone

00:39:57.646 --> 00:40:00.130
is higher for the peak-hours

00:40:00.131 --> 00:40:01.470
than it is for
the non-peak hours,

00:40:01.471 --> 00:40:03.790
and the welfare-maximizing
price is

00:40:03.791 --> 00:40:06.130
lower during those non-peak hours

00:40:06.131 --> 00:40:07.890
than it is during the peak hours.

00:40:07.891 --> 00:40:10.600
So, the dynamic pricing systems,

00:40:10.601 --> 00:40:14.940
specifically has to keep

00:40:14.941 --> 00:40:18.155
the price above this danger zone.

00:40:18.156 --> 00:40:19.900
&gt;&gt; If you do it, you think

00:40:19.901 --> 00:40:21.560
it's so fragile and
brittle that you want to

00:40:21.561 --> 00:40:26.075
be robust to the variation

00:40:26.076 --> 00:40:27.990
between your model and reality.

00:40:27.991 --> 00:40:31.900
So, do you do active monitoring
to see where you are,

00:40:31.901 --> 00:40:34.255
or do you actually try to robust

00:40:34.256 --> 00:40:37.460
estimates away from the glyphs?

00:40:37.461 --> 00:40:41.490
&gt;&gt; So, I think you're
absolutely correct that there's

00:40:41.491 --> 00:40:44.025
a certain amount
of modeling noise

00:40:44.026 --> 00:40:45.750
that once you take into account,

00:40:45.751 --> 00:40:48.195
this is playing
right on the edge.

00:40:48.196 --> 00:40:51.460
Again, this isn't
Uber's surge pricing system,

00:40:51.461 --> 00:40:54.080
it's just an illustration
of the concept.

00:40:54.081 --> 00:40:54.880
&gt;&gt; Got it.

00:40:54.881 --> 00:40:56.380
&gt;&gt; But, yes, in practice,

00:40:56.381 --> 00:40:57.520
if you were to implement this,

00:40:57.521 --> 00:41:00.160
you would want to take
that into account. Yes?

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:02.460
&gt;&gt; Is it safe to assume

00:41:02.461 --> 00:41:06.070
that is what is the opportunity
cost for the driver?

00:41:06.071 --> 00:41:08.930
So any multiple less than one,

00:41:08.931 --> 00:41:10.300
the driver might be losing

00:41:10.301 --> 00:41:13.795
money compared to
doing something else?

00:41:13.796 --> 00:41:16.900
&gt;&gt; So, certainly,
the sensitivity of

00:41:16.901 --> 00:41:20.155
supply to earnings is part of
what went into this model.

00:41:20.156 --> 00:41:22.185
But it's not just the driver.

00:41:22.186 --> 00:41:23.550
This is not just

00:41:23.551 --> 00:41:26.734
the fact that drivers
have an alternative.

00:41:26.735 --> 00:41:30.115
It's due to the fact that
this pickup time phenomenon,

00:41:30.116 --> 00:41:33.220
where the pickup times
get really high,

00:41:33.221 --> 00:41:34.585
and so the drivers are not

00:41:34.586 --> 00:41:36.460
earning money during
those long pickup times.

00:41:36.461 --> 00:41:38.500
It has more to do with
that than it does to do

00:41:38.501 --> 00:41:41.830
with the drivers' alternatives.

00:41:42.830 --> 00:41:46.300
So, one upshot of this is

00:41:46.301 --> 00:41:49.270
that if dynamic
pricing is disallowed,

00:41:49.271 --> 00:41:50.880
meaning that
the same search multiple

00:41:50.881 --> 00:41:52.730
has to be used for
all times and locations,

00:41:52.731 --> 00:41:55.960
the welfare maximizing
fixed multiple is

00:41:55.961 --> 00:42:00.010
right up here with the dynamic
price for the peak-hours.

00:42:00.011 --> 00:42:01.950
The system would end up setting

00:42:01.951 --> 00:42:04.470
a higher price if dynamic
pricing is not allowed,

00:42:04.471 --> 00:42:06.480
which means that fewer trips are

00:42:06.481 --> 00:42:09.500
created and less
welfare is created.

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:13.220
So this is why dynamic pricing is

00:42:13.221 --> 00:42:16.380
important in
ride-hailing platforms.

00:42:16.760 --> 00:42:19.495
Okay, so I'm going to

00:42:19.496 --> 00:42:21.340
talk a little bit about how
to estimate the inputs,

00:42:21.341 --> 00:42:23.150
but I also want to
have time to talk

00:42:23.151 --> 00:42:25.855
about alternative
matching algorithms.

00:42:25.856 --> 00:42:28.310
Were we on time, Eric?

00:42:28.311 --> 00:42:32.020
&gt;&gt; I think we're running
a little short today.

00:42:32.021 --> 00:42:34.000
&gt;&gt; A little short?

00:42:34.001 --> 00:42:35.320
Okay.

00:42:35.321 --> 00:42:38.350
&gt;&gt; Can we maybe settle
that later on accounting?

00:42:38.351 --> 00:42:38.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:42:38.771 --> 00:42:39.415
&gt;&gt; Okay, Esther?

00:42:39.416 --> 00:42:39.955
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:42:39.956 --> 00:42:41.550
&gt;&gt; How about 12-15 minutes?

00:42:41.551 --> 00:42:43.750
&gt;&gt; Sounds great.
Thank you. All right,

00:42:43.751 --> 00:42:46.540
so let's come back to
this demand model,

00:42:46.541 --> 00:42:49.210
and remember that lambda here is

00:42:49.211 --> 00:42:52.580
the arrival rate for
rider sessions over time,

00:42:52.581 --> 00:42:58.060
and part time, and
geographic unit.

00:42:58.061 --> 00:42:59.930
So we have to be
able to predict what

00:42:59.931 --> 00:43:02.340
lambda is at least in
the very short term.

00:43:02.341 --> 00:43:04.420
In the next five minutes,
in the next 10 minutes,

00:43:04.421 --> 00:43:06.035
how many riders do we expect to

00:43:06.036 --> 00:43:08.310
open the app and
check their price?

00:43:08.311 --> 00:43:09.970
I'm going to illustrate this with

00:43:09.971 --> 00:43:11.230
New York City taxi data,

00:43:11.231 --> 00:43:12.555
which is publicly available.

00:43:12.556 --> 00:43:15.040
That's because demand-data and

00:43:15.041 --> 00:43:19.040
geolocation for demand is
sensitive for privacy reasons.

00:43:19.041 --> 00:43:25.160
But you can see that
in this type of data,

00:43:25.161 --> 00:43:26.890
you get these very strong
geographic patterns.

00:43:26.891 --> 00:43:27.960
This is Manhattan.

00:43:27.961 --> 00:43:30.040
You can see much higher demand

00:43:30.041 --> 00:43:31.930
in Manhattan than in
surrounding areas,

00:43:31.931 --> 00:43:33.805
and higher demand in

00:43:33.806 --> 00:43:38.390
the peak-times than in
the middle of the night.

00:43:38.391 --> 00:43:45.340
So, here's a simple illustration

00:43:45.341 --> 00:43:48.580
of short-term prediction of

00:43:48.581 --> 00:43:51.020
this demands by time
and geolocation.

00:43:51.021 --> 00:43:53.185
In this case, the authors used

00:43:53.186 --> 00:43:57.550
a random forest model with
discretization of latitude,

00:43:57.551 --> 00:43:59.720
and longitude, and time with

00:43:59.721 --> 00:44:04.585
a remarkable degree of success
for a simple approach.

00:44:04.586 --> 00:44:08.060
So, we're showing here the
prediction on the left,

00:44:08.061 --> 00:44:09.810
and the actual on the right,

00:44:09.811 --> 00:44:10.830
and you can see they basically

00:44:10.831 --> 00:44:12.310
captures the critical features.

00:44:12.311 --> 00:44:15.850
There's more density of demand
in these downtown areas.

00:44:15.851 --> 00:44:17.335
It's even able to pick up,

00:44:17.336 --> 00:44:18.830
the fact that there's more demand

00:44:18.831 --> 00:44:20.100
along the thoroughfares in

00:44:20.101 --> 00:44:22.290
Manhattan and is able

00:44:22.291 --> 00:44:24.430
to capture those daily
cycles effectively.

00:44:24.431 --> 00:44:33.420
The input to
the matching algorithms

00:44:33.421 --> 00:44:34.860
was this travel time prediction.

00:44:34.861 --> 00:44:37.330
Let's just talk about
that for a moment.

00:44:37.331 --> 00:44:39.490
So, what does the data look like

00:44:39.491 --> 00:44:41.610
for this type of prediction?

00:44:41.611 --> 00:44:46.910
The prediction is done using
location data from trips.

00:44:46.911 --> 00:44:49.030
So when a driver has
a passenger in the vehicle,

00:44:49.031 --> 00:44:52.605
we have GPS data
during that trip,

00:44:52.606 --> 00:44:56.155
and that's shown in red
for a particular example,

00:44:56.156 --> 00:44:57.670
the sequence of points

00:44:57.671 --> 00:44:59.360
starting from top
left to top right.

00:44:59.361 --> 00:45:03.770
You can see that in this case,

00:45:03.771 --> 00:45:05.350
it looks like the driver
went through a tunnel.

00:45:05.351 --> 00:45:07.030
There's one GPS point that has

00:45:07.031 --> 00:45:10.100
either very large noise in
the latitude and longitude,

00:45:10.101 --> 00:45:12.410
or an incorrect timestamp.

00:45:12.411 --> 00:45:14.750
So, certainly,
any algorithms will

00:45:14.751 --> 00:45:17.680
need to be robust to that noise.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:21.630
So, how might one do

00:45:21.631 --> 00:45:24.755
travel-time prediction
using this type of data?

00:45:24.756 --> 00:45:27.885
The first step is what's
called map-matching,

00:45:27.886 --> 00:45:32.125
and John has some wonderful
algorithms for this.

00:45:32.126 --> 00:45:36.435
Shown in blue is
the map-matched trip, meaning,

00:45:36.436 --> 00:45:39.375
the estimated route and

00:45:39.376 --> 00:45:41.080
we also have to
estimate the amount of

00:45:41.081 --> 00:45:43.010
time that the driver
took to traverse

00:45:43.011 --> 00:45:45.700
each road segment in that route.

00:45:45.701 --> 00:45:49.635
And you can see that
this case it did pretty well,

00:45:49.636 --> 00:45:57.690
it estimated that the driver
went through the tunnel.

00:45:57.691 --> 00:46:01.000
Okay, after this
data-processing step,

00:46:01.001 --> 00:46:04.710
then, you can aggregate by
the road segment level.

00:46:04.711 --> 00:46:06.590
So here, the histograms here,

00:46:06.591 --> 00:46:07.710
these are four histograms for

00:46:07.711 --> 00:46:09.230
four particular road segments.

00:46:09.231 --> 00:46:14.460
This is actually data
from the project at MSR.

00:46:15.380 --> 00:46:18.390
For this particular road
segment, for example,

00:46:18.391 --> 00:46:20.335
this is looking at all of

00:46:20.336 --> 00:46:22.160
the traversals of
this road segment

00:46:22.161 --> 00:46:23.575
during evening rush hour.

00:46:23.576 --> 00:46:25.710
&gt;&gt; This is 520. I think
it's 520, isn't it?

00:46:25.711 --> 00:46:27.570
&gt;&gt; It's something
like that, yeah.

00:46:27.571 --> 00:46:30.595
&gt;&gt; But we all love 520. This
is like the old bridge.

00:46:30.596 --> 00:46:32.540
&gt;&gt; These are
the four most commonly

00:46:32.541 --> 00:46:35.380
traversed highway road
segments in Seattle,

00:46:35.381 --> 00:46:37.600
I don't remember exactly.

00:46:37.601 --> 00:46:41.280
And then, the curve
is a model that can

00:46:41.281 --> 00:46:46.120
be fit to travel speeds at
the road segment level.

00:46:46.121 --> 00:46:48.880
This is some particular
model that we use

00:46:48.881 --> 00:46:50.720
for this project
but more generally,

00:46:50.721 --> 00:46:53.090
you can think about
creating a model at

00:46:53.091 --> 00:46:55.900
the road segment level for

00:46:55.901 --> 00:46:59.955
predicting the travel time
across that road segment.

00:46:59.956 --> 00:47:01.930
Given a set of predictors,

00:47:01.931 --> 00:47:03.840
things like what is

00:47:03.841 --> 00:47:05.010
the historical traffic for

00:47:05.011 --> 00:47:06.790
this road segment at
this time of week?

00:47:06.791 --> 00:47:09.620
What's the historical travel
time for this time of week?

00:47:09.621 --> 00:47:12.290
What's the last 15 minutes

00:47:12.291 --> 00:47:13.880
average for this road segment

00:47:13.881 --> 00:47:15.730
in terms of the travel time?

00:47:15.731 --> 00:47:18.920
How's the weather? Is it
a holiday, and so on?

00:47:18.921 --> 00:47:21.359
So, there's typically a model

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:24.415
for road segment
traversals times,

00:47:24.416 --> 00:47:28.190
and then those individual
predictions are going to be put

00:47:28.191 --> 00:47:30.500
together to predict the amount of

00:47:30.501 --> 00:47:31.820
travel time between an origin

00:47:31.821 --> 00:47:34.145
and a destination in a moment.

00:47:34.146 --> 00:47:38.070
Great. So now that we have
these segment level models,

00:47:38.071 --> 00:47:41.360
you can then predict in real time

00:47:41.361 --> 00:47:43.830
the travel time on

00:47:43.831 --> 00:47:45.900
any particular road segment
in the road network.

00:47:45.901 --> 00:47:48.225
Here, this is showing

00:47:48.226 --> 00:47:51.910
predicted travel speed for

00:47:51.911 --> 00:47:54.140
the road segments in
the San Francisco Bay Area,

00:47:54.141 --> 00:47:56.355
overlaid on a
satellite photograph.

00:47:56.356 --> 00:47:59.250
Green means faster
and red means slower,

00:47:59.251 --> 00:48:00.560
and you can see that the highways

00:48:00.561 --> 00:48:01.780
have higher predicted speed in

00:48:01.781 --> 00:48:05.720
this example than downtown
San Francisco side roads.

00:48:05.721 --> 00:48:07.450
This is probably
the middle of the night;

00:48:07.451 --> 00:48:09.120
this is an uncongested
time period.

00:48:09.121 --> 00:48:13.070
&gt;&gt; Can you share anything

00:48:13.071 --> 00:48:17.160
about what you actually prefer
for picking road speeds?

00:48:17.161 --> 00:48:18.630
&gt;&gt; For traffic prediction?

00:48:18.631 --> 00:48:23.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah. Is the probe network

00:48:23.181 --> 00:48:25.340
from Uber itself good
enough to do predictions?

00:48:25.341 --> 00:48:27.465
Is not taken multiple sources of

00:48:27.466 --> 00:48:30.040
data like we did with
them for Clear Flow?

00:48:30.041 --> 00:48:31.710
You don't have to

00:48:31.711 --> 00:48:33.195
answer if you can't
answer that question.

00:48:33.196 --> 00:48:35.840
&gt;&gt; Sure. Maybe let's
talk offline about it.

00:48:35.841 --> 00:48:37.440
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:48:37.441 --> 00:48:39.020
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:48:39.021 --> 00:48:40.915
The primary data source would be

00:48:40.916 --> 00:48:42.420
the east traversal times for

00:48:42.421 --> 00:48:44.260
vehicles on the road network.

00:48:44.261 --> 00:48:46.450
&gt;&gt; But then that's
how you generalize

00:48:46.451 --> 00:48:48.680
that signals where
they're not running, so?

00:48:48.681 --> 00:48:49.860
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:48:49.861 --> 00:48:51.365
&gt;&gt; You have a clear flow answer.

00:48:51.366 --> 00:48:56.315
&gt;&gt; Okay, so Eric's pointing
out the fact that using

00:48:56.316 --> 00:48:58.780
recent data is a great way to

00:48:58.781 --> 00:49:01.610
predict traffic if you're
talking about a highway.

00:49:01.611 --> 00:49:02.960
It doesn't work so
well in some of

00:49:02.961 --> 00:49:05.010
these areas with very
sparse data and,

00:49:05.011 --> 00:49:07.180
so a lot of the trick and
traffic modeling is how to

00:49:07.181 --> 00:49:09.060
deal with traffic modeling

00:49:09.061 --> 00:49:10.560
for the areas with sparse data,

00:49:10.561 --> 00:49:11.640
and that's absolutely correct.

00:49:11.641 --> 00:49:14.895
So, let's talk about it later.

00:49:14.896 --> 00:49:19.220
Great. So then you can put
together these individual

00:49:19.221 --> 00:49:20.840
travel times to get travel time

00:49:20.841 --> 00:49:23.135
from point A to point B
in the following fashion.

00:49:23.136 --> 00:49:25.560
The next step is to compute

00:49:25.561 --> 00:49:30.040
the fastest path based on
those traffic predictions.

00:49:32.060 --> 00:49:34.930
There's a ton of
different algorithms for this,

00:49:34.931 --> 00:49:39.330
like A star, and Hub labeling
algorithms and so on.

00:49:39.530 --> 00:49:42.140
Then you could simply
sum the travel

00:49:42.141 --> 00:49:45.670
times across that route.

00:49:45.671 --> 00:49:46.915
You could stop there,

00:49:46.916 --> 00:49:49.865
it turns out that this
doesn't work perfectly,

00:49:49.866 --> 00:49:51.495
because you tend to underpredict

00:49:51.496 --> 00:49:53.335
the amount of travel time
on the route.

00:49:53.336 --> 00:49:55.790
Why is that? Any guesses?

00:49:55.791 --> 00:49:56.915
&gt;&gt; Stop lights.

00:49:56.916 --> 00:49:59.260
&gt;&gt; Stop lights, but that
should be incorporated,

00:49:59.261 --> 00:50:00.770
that's a great call out.

00:50:00.771 --> 00:50:01.900
Potentially stop lights we try to

00:50:01.901 --> 00:50:03.525
incorporate that into the models,

00:50:03.526 --> 00:50:05.390
but yes that's an issue.

00:50:05.391 --> 00:50:07.235
&gt;&gt; Dynamically changing.

00:50:07.236 --> 00:50:10.520
&gt;&gt; Dynamic, okay so
changes over time.

00:50:10.521 --> 00:50:13.170
So the issue is that there's

00:50:13.171 --> 00:50:15.720
a consistent
underprediction effect.

00:50:15.721 --> 00:50:18.850
If we first find

00:50:18.851 --> 00:50:21.200
the fastest route and then
predict the travel time for

00:50:21.201 --> 00:50:22.480
that route and that's
because drivers

00:50:22.481 --> 00:50:24.800
don't always take
the fastest route.

00:50:25.990 --> 00:50:28.510
So there's an
underprediction bias,

00:50:28.511 --> 00:50:30.210
and so typically at
this stage there's

00:50:30.211 --> 00:50:32.770
some kind of a bias adjustment or

00:50:32.771 --> 00:50:35.230
a second model to account

00:50:35.231 --> 00:50:36.290
for the fact the drivers are

00:50:36.291 --> 00:50:38.720
not always taking
the fastest path.

00:50:40.500 --> 00:50:43.500
So it can be simple as
a bias adjustment but

00:50:43.501 --> 00:50:45.310
again more sophisticated models

00:50:45.311 --> 00:50:47.340
are possible at this stage.

00:50:48.260 --> 00:50:51.540
So I do want to come back to

00:50:51.541 --> 00:50:56.150
this concept of
novel matching algorithms,

00:50:56.151 --> 00:50:58.900
and how might they
help in mitigating

00:50:58.901 --> 00:51:01.220
the variability of price

00:51:01.221 --> 00:51:03.250
that's introduced
through dynamic pricing.

00:51:03.251 --> 00:51:07.820
But here's a simple mechanism.

00:51:08.100 --> 00:51:11.930
Let's think about choosing
a waiting window,

00:51:11.931 --> 00:51:13.960
call that capital fee,

00:51:13.961 --> 00:51:17.470
that's going to be
on the rider side.

00:51:17.471 --> 00:51:19.270
This fee could be dynamic,

00:51:19.271 --> 00:51:22.520
it could depend on supply and
demand conditions.

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:25.730
So when a rider
requests a ride instead

00:51:25.731 --> 00:51:29.010
of only looking at the open cars,

00:51:29.011 --> 00:51:30.805
we're going to consider
both the cars that are

00:51:30.806 --> 00:51:33.990
open and the cars that
are currently on-trip.

00:51:33.991 --> 00:51:36.920
All the on-trip drivers

00:51:36.921 --> 00:51:38.920
within few minutes of
completing their trip.

00:51:38.921 --> 00:51:42.855
So we're going to expand
the supply base in that sense.

00:51:42.856 --> 00:51:46.990
We're going to dispatch the
driver who minimizes either,

00:51:46.991 --> 00:51:50.910
the time to pick up if
that's an open driver,

00:51:50.911 --> 00:51:55.380
or if it's a driver
that's currently on-trip,

00:51:55.381 --> 00:51:57.955
the amount of time
between their drop-off

00:51:57.956 --> 00:52:01.715
and the location of
that next rider.

00:52:01.716 --> 00:52:04.250
So let's illustrate
that with an example.

00:52:04.251 --> 00:52:06.570
So here's a rider, there's
four possible cars,

00:52:06.571 --> 00:52:08.000
two of which are currently open

00:52:08.001 --> 00:52:10.060
and two of which are
currently on trip.

00:52:10.061 --> 00:52:11.960
So this car is five minutes from

00:52:11.961 --> 00:52:14.175
the rider so we call
that five minutes.

00:52:14.176 --> 00:52:17.105
Eight minutes here,
and on this side,

00:52:17.106 --> 00:52:20.535
this is a card that's four
minutes from its drop off.

00:52:20.536 --> 00:52:24.475
The drop off location is two
minutes from this rider,

00:52:24.476 --> 00:52:26.200
and so we're going
to count that as two

00:52:26.201 --> 00:52:28.485
minutes not a six minutes.

00:52:28.486 --> 00:52:32.590
Then similarly for
this potential match,

00:52:32.591 --> 00:52:34.875
we're going to count
the five minutes

00:52:34.876 --> 00:52:36.410
and not the seven total minutes,

00:52:36.411 --> 00:52:38.140
and then we're going to
try to minimize that.

00:52:38.141 --> 00:52:41.980
So in this case we would
dispatch driver C,

00:52:41.981 --> 00:52:44.550
because they minimize
the time between

00:52:44.551 --> 00:52:46.570
this drop-off location
and the pickup.

00:52:46.571 --> 00:52:49.100
So why this mechanism?

00:52:52.710 --> 00:52:57.410
Notice that this is a
dynamic waiting mechanism.

00:52:57.411 --> 00:53:00.130
It's asking drivers
to wait a little bit

00:53:00.131 --> 00:53:01.840
longer instead of or in

00:53:01.841 --> 00:53:04.415
addition to paying
a higher price.

00:53:04.416 --> 00:53:07.610
You could even think about
dynamically adjusting

00:53:07.611 --> 00:53:09.190
that wait time and dynamically

00:53:09.191 --> 00:53:10.740
adjusting the price together.

00:53:10.741 --> 00:53:15.200
But the critical thing

00:53:15.201 --> 00:53:17.590
to understand about
this mechanism is it's basically

00:53:17.591 --> 00:53:21.100
trying to avoid
the danger zone by

00:53:21.101 --> 00:53:24.260
optimizing for less wasted time

00:53:24.261 --> 00:53:26.710
between trips for drivers.

00:53:27.010 --> 00:53:29.230
So it's asking the rider to

00:53:29.231 --> 00:53:31.155
flex a little bit in
terms of their time,

00:53:31.156 --> 00:53:33.650
but it's minimizing the amount
of time that drivers

00:53:33.651 --> 00:53:39.020
spend between on their way
to pick up riders.

00:53:40.880 --> 00:53:44.150
This is a similar
concept to some of

00:53:44.151 --> 00:53:46.860
the flexing ideas in
one of Uber's products,

00:53:46.861 --> 00:53:48.400
the Express Pool product,

00:53:48.401 --> 00:53:50.010
where riders are asked to wait

00:53:50.011 --> 00:53:52.470
a little bit longer before
they dispatch a driver,

00:53:52.471 --> 00:53:54.370
and are also asked to walk

00:53:54.371 --> 00:53:57.290
a little bit to
meet their driver.

00:53:58.920 --> 00:54:01.240
So what happens? This is

00:54:01.241 --> 00:54:04.400
simulation for downtown
San Francisco,

00:54:04.401 --> 00:54:07.320
but it's based on actual demand

00:54:07.321 --> 00:54:09.705
and supply data from this geo.

00:54:09.706 --> 00:54:11.450
In blue what I'm showing is

00:54:11.451 --> 00:54:15.005
the optimal dynamic waiting time,

00:54:15.006 --> 00:54:21.025
if we allow dynamic waiting
and dynamic pricing.

00:54:21.026 --> 00:54:25.270
In red I'm showing the optimal
dynamic waiting time

00:54:25.271 --> 00:54:27.400
if we don't allow
dynamic pricing.

00:54:27.401 --> 00:54:30.860
You can see that under
the scenario where you have

00:54:30.861 --> 00:54:34.425
both dynamic waiting
and dynamic pricing,

00:54:34.426 --> 00:54:36.960
you have pretty
reasonable wait times,

00:54:36.961 --> 00:54:38.720
anywhere between zero
and four minutes.

00:54:38.721 --> 00:54:40.195
So not super high,

00:54:40.196 --> 00:54:41.990
these riders are being
asked to wait up to

00:54:41.991 --> 00:54:44.440
four minutes longer than
they normally would.

00:54:44.441 --> 00:54:47.110
This scenario where
dynamic pricing is not

00:54:47.111 --> 00:54:49.890
allowed leads to very
long rider wait times,

00:54:49.891 --> 00:54:53.790
probably not going to work
from a product perspective.

00:54:54.470 --> 00:54:56.990
So let's go back to that curve

00:54:56.991 --> 00:54:58.390
that we looked at before where

00:54:58.391 --> 00:54:59.820
we were looking at welfare as

00:54:59.821 --> 00:55:01.510
a function of
the price multiplier.

00:55:01.511 --> 00:55:04.240
So you saw this
blue curve before,

00:55:04.241 --> 00:55:05.915
although I think it
was a different color,

00:55:05.916 --> 00:55:08.680
and this is the curve
that showed how

00:55:08.681 --> 00:55:10.530
the danger zone effects

00:55:10.531 --> 00:55:13.515
shows up as a function
of the surge multiplier.

00:55:13.516 --> 00:55:15.220
So this is the curve for welfare

00:55:15.221 --> 00:55:16.765
as a function of
the surge multiplier,

00:55:16.766 --> 00:55:19.185
and the blue curve
is the case where

00:55:19.186 --> 00:55:22.740
there's dynamic pricing but
not dynamic rider waiting,

00:55:22.741 --> 00:55:26.065
and the red curve is the case
where both are included.

00:55:26.066 --> 00:55:28.680
You can see that
the introduction of

00:55:28.681 --> 00:55:30.280
this dynamic rider waiting

00:55:30.281 --> 00:55:33.365
moves the danger zone
to the left.

00:55:33.366 --> 00:55:37.465
So, reduces the optimal value of

00:55:37.466 --> 00:55:39.300
the surge multiplier and also

00:55:39.301 --> 00:55:42.430
slightly increases
the total welfare created.

00:55:42.431 --> 00:55:44.585
So if you look at

00:55:44.586 --> 00:55:46.140
the optimal search multiplier as

00:55:46.141 --> 00:55:48.235
a function of the hour of day,

00:55:48.236 --> 00:55:51.925
the blue curve is the case
without the rider waiting,

00:55:51.926 --> 00:55:54.460
and the red curve is the case
with the rider waiting.

00:55:54.461 --> 00:55:57.460
You can see that the surge
multiples are generally lower,

00:55:57.461 --> 00:56:00.120
and the variability is less
than the surge multiple from

00:56:00.121 --> 00:56:03.695
one time period to the next.

00:56:03.696 --> 00:56:07.560
Any questions? Yeah.

00:56:07.561 --> 00:56:09.820
&gt;&gt; I have a general
question about

00:56:09.821 --> 00:56:12.470
the predictability
of this system.

00:56:12.471 --> 00:56:15.210
So let's say you have
two consecutive days with

00:56:15.211 --> 00:56:17.000
exactly the same
conditions in terms

00:56:17.001 --> 00:56:19.205
of demand and all that.

00:56:19.206 --> 00:56:22.150
Would the prices
behave similarly,

00:56:22.151 --> 00:56:24.220
or the dynamics would
behave similarly?

00:56:24.221 --> 00:56:26.945
Maybe think of the notion
of equilibrium in

00:56:26.946 --> 00:56:28.690
other multiple equilibria or

00:56:28.691 --> 00:56:31.950
a single equilibrium in
these kind of scenarios.

00:56:31.951 --> 00:56:34.860
&gt;&gt; So, single equilibrium

00:56:34.861 --> 00:56:37.660
given fixed supply and
demand conditions,

00:56:37.661 --> 00:56:39.320
but the supply and
demand conditions do

00:56:39.321 --> 00:56:41.890
fluctuate a fair amount
even day to day.

00:56:41.891 --> 00:56:45.870
If you look at the the
short-term prediction of demand,

00:56:45.871 --> 00:56:52.300
in fact you get
these local fluctuations

00:56:52.301 --> 00:56:54.015
in supply and demand.

00:56:54.016 --> 00:56:56.000
&gt;&gt; Exactly the same demand and

00:56:56.001 --> 00:56:58.190
supply across two different days.

00:56:58.191 --> 00:57:00.650
&gt;&gt;Then you'll get
the same surge price,

00:57:00.651 --> 00:57:01.880
because the surge price is

00:57:01.881 --> 00:57:03.405
a function of
the supply and demand.

00:57:03.406 --> 00:57:06.040
What am I not understanding?
Oh you're saying

00:57:06.041 --> 00:57:09.015
because the drivers respond-

00:57:09.016 --> 00:57:12.390
&gt;&gt; Yes. I'm thinking about

00:57:12.391 --> 00:57:17.495
the notion in Nash setting
like unique equilibrium,

00:57:17.496 --> 00:57:19.825
is there unique equilibrium
in this kind of setting?

00:57:19.826 --> 00:57:22.780
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, for
a given price point there

00:57:22.781 --> 00:57:26.485
is an equilibrium value
for the waiting time,

00:57:26.486 --> 00:57:29.240
under fixed supply and
demand conditions.

00:57:29.241 --> 00:57:29.720
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:57:29.721 --> 00:57:30.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:57:31.060 --> 00:57:32.920
There's a theorem about that,

00:57:32.921 --> 00:57:33.870
I didn't include it in the talk.

00:57:33.871 --> 00:57:35.440
Great.

00:57:35.441 --> 00:57:42.250
So, to conclude this growth

00:57:42.251 --> 00:57:44.440
of ride-hailing is facilitated

00:57:44.441 --> 00:57:47.285
by these matching and
pricing algorithms.

00:57:47.286 --> 00:57:49.970
These algorithms
reduce the wasted time

00:57:49.971 --> 00:57:52.770
for both riders and for drivers.

00:57:52.771 --> 00:57:55.590
They require forecasts of demand,

00:57:55.591 --> 00:57:57.070
of supply, and travel time.

00:57:57.071 --> 00:58:00.545
We talked about how one might
generate those forecasts.

00:58:00.546 --> 00:58:03.935
We also talked about
how introducing

00:58:03.936 --> 00:58:06.575
different matching algorithms
might be able to help

00:58:06.576 --> 00:58:08.180
decrease the price variability

00:58:08.181 --> 00:58:10.200
associated with dynamic pricing.

00:58:10.201 --> 00:58:12.710
Then, there's this meta point
around the fact that this

00:58:12.711 --> 00:58:15.280
isn't just machine learning
that we're doing.

00:58:15.281 --> 00:58:17.785
It's a mix of economic models,

00:58:17.786 --> 00:58:20.350
statistical approaches,
machine learning,

00:58:20.351 --> 00:58:21.400
and optimization.

00:58:21.401 --> 00:58:28.570
Thanks very much.

00:58:28.571 --> 00:58:31.330
&gt;&gt; We're up for a few
questions. Yeah, go ahead.

00:58:31.331 --> 00:58:33.750
&gt;&gt; So, regarding
the new matching you showed,

00:58:33.751 --> 00:58:35.030
so where you look at

00:58:35.031 --> 00:58:38.450
the drop-off time and you
measure only the distance.

00:58:38.451 --> 00:58:41.840
So is it the entire algorithm
or there is a cutoff?

00:58:41.841 --> 00:58:44.235
Because maybe there
is a drop off.

00:58:44.236 --> 00:58:47.095
&gt;&gt; The cutoff is capital is Phi,

00:58:47.096 --> 00:58:49.395
so that's what we're flexing.

00:58:49.396 --> 00:58:50.740
Is the maximum amount of

00:58:50.741 --> 00:58:53.440
extra waiting that we would
require from the rider.

00:58:53.441 --> 00:58:55.450
&gt;&gt; The second question I
had was more open-ended.

00:58:55.451 --> 00:58:58.625
I'm thinking like in
terms of the drivers,

00:58:58.626 --> 00:59:00.755
is there in our data,

00:59:00.756 --> 00:59:03.590
what is the amount of
money made by each driver?

00:59:03.591 --> 00:59:06.550
Is it fair across
all the drivers or?

00:59:06.551 --> 00:59:10.270
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, fairness of
earnings across drivers.

00:59:10.271 --> 00:59:11.610
&gt;&gt; Yeah, because
these policies can determine

00:59:11.611 --> 00:59:14.210
which driver will be
dispatched to the customer.

00:59:14.211 --> 00:59:16.830
So, and it explicitly
does not take into

00:59:16.831 --> 00:59:20.430
account the "fairness" of
the drivers or something.

00:59:20.431 --> 00:59:22.250
&gt;&gt; So, it depends on what
you mean by fairness.

00:59:22.251 --> 00:59:22.775
&gt;&gt; In terms of the-

00:59:22.776 --> 00:59:24.940
&gt;&gt; These are fair mechanisms in

00:59:24.941 --> 00:59:28.035
the sense that nothing is
specific to that driver, right?

00:59:28.036 --> 00:59:30.410
But, the mechanisms discussed

00:59:30.411 --> 00:59:31.880
here are all mechanisms that

00:59:31.881 --> 00:59:34.120
have only to do with the distance

00:59:34.121 --> 00:59:36.615
between a rider and
a driver, for example.

00:59:36.616 --> 00:59:38.385
They're not personalized
in any sense.

00:59:38.386 --> 00:59:40.305
So, they're fair in that sense.

00:59:40.306 --> 00:59:43.010
Fairness is perhaps
a longer discussion

00:59:43.011 --> 00:59:44.050
if you wanted to say, "Okay,

00:59:44.051 --> 00:59:46.710
if you're trying to
create earnings that

00:59:46.711 --> 00:59:49.380
are as similar across
drivers as possible,

00:59:49.381 --> 00:59:50.730
and to reduce variability

00:59:50.731 --> 00:59:53.330
across drivers as
much as possible."

00:59:53.331 --> 00:59:55.250
That's an interesting question

00:59:55.251 --> 00:59:58.550
and a longer one to address.

00:59:58.551 --> 01:00:03.520
&gt;&gt; Like the data which says
like roughly speaking,

01:00:03.521 --> 01:00:05.780
they make the average amount
of money made for

01:00:05.781 --> 01:00:08.865
the driver is roughly
the same, or something.

01:00:08.866 --> 01:00:10.860
&gt;&gt; There's some data for that

01:00:10.861 --> 01:00:13.405
publicly available in papers.

01:00:13.406 --> 01:00:15.515
I don't have that on
the tip of my tongue.

01:00:15.516 --> 01:00:16.790
&gt;&gt; Do you have
a question over here?

01:00:16.791 --> 01:00:18.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I had a question

01:00:18.271 --> 01:00:20.330
about how do you
assess the demand?

01:00:20.331 --> 01:00:23.400
Say if I'm just opening my app
into new prices, right?

01:00:23.401 --> 01:00:24.780
I'm not requesting it,

01:00:24.781 --> 01:00:26.450
does that go into the model of,

01:00:26.451 --> 01:00:29.070
does it categorize as demand?

01:00:29.071 --> 01:00:31.240
&gt;&gt; Yes, so that's
exactly what Lambda

01:00:31.241 --> 01:00:33.580
was in this model for.

01:00:33.581 --> 01:00:39.770
So, predicting demand, so we
basically assume the number

01:00:39.771 --> 01:00:42.120
of riders opening the app
and checking the price

01:00:42.121 --> 01:00:45.930
is just a function of
time and geolocation,

01:00:45.931 --> 01:00:48.530
it's not a function of
what price I search multiple,

01:00:48.531 --> 01:00:50.000
we happen to have right now and

01:00:50.001 --> 01:00:51.470
then we assume that the chance of

01:00:51.471 --> 01:00:53.830
the rider requests after
they're showing the price does

01:00:53.831 --> 01:00:55.080
depend on what price you showed

01:00:55.081 --> 01:00:56.410
them, that's how
we break it down.

01:00:56.411 --> 01:00:59.480
So, then this prediction,

01:00:59.481 --> 01:01:01.150
you have this prediction
of number of riders

01:01:01.151 --> 01:01:02.960
opening the app and
checking the price, yes.

01:01:02.961 --> 01:01:04.460
&gt;&gt; Just like, write a script

01:01:04.461 --> 01:01:06.080
which keeps pinging the service,

01:01:06.081 --> 01:01:07.730
does that mean the prices for

01:01:07.731 --> 01:01:10.100
everyone else around
me will come?

01:01:10.101 --> 01:01:13.070
&gt;&gt; So, we're aware that
people could do this and

01:01:13.071 --> 01:01:14.310
so we have safeguards in

01:01:14.311 --> 01:01:17.180
place to avoid them,
specifically this.

01:01:17.880 --> 01:01:20.140
&gt;&gt; So, it seems
